{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310840,"nonce":"ipf7yQJsPI0c+0lshBZpls8M+5g=","title":"Phase (c) of the llama-chat coherence push. Three substantive fixes landed: (1) compiler-level enum-variant disambiguation by expected arg ...","message":"`compiler/src/tast/ast_lowering.rs`:\n- New stack `expected_arg_type_stack: Vec\u003cOption\u003cTypeId\u003e\u003e` parallel to the existing `expected_lambda_params_stack`.\n- `lower_call_expression` pushes the formal param TypeId before lowering each arg.\n- `lower_expression(Ident(name))` checks the top of the stack when the scope-walk resolved an enum variant whose parent enum differs from the expected enum. If the expected type is `Enum E` and the variant doesn't belong to E, re-resolve the name within E's variants.\n\nVerified directly:\n```haxe\nenum LocalE { F32; Other(v:Int); }\nfunction takesDType(d:DType):Int return 1;\nfunction takesLocalE(e:LocalE):Int return 2;\n\ntakesDType(F32);    // → 1  (resolves to DType.F32)\ntakesLocalE(F32);   // → 2  (resolves to LocalE.F32)\nTensor.zeros([4], F32).dtype();  // → 0 (F32)\n```\n\nThis removes the `DType.F32` qualification workaround that the original [bugs_dtype_enum_cross_file_pointer] memory recommended. Reverted `Linear.hx` and `GQAttention.hx` back to bare `F32` to exercise the new disambiguation.\n\n\n`runtime/src/tensor.rs:rayzor_tensor_rope` was using interleaved `(x[2i], x[2i+1])` pairs — the older GPT-style RoPE. Llama 3 (and Mistral, Qwen, Phi) use **half-split** `(x[i], x[i+half])`. Reference: `transformers/models/llama/modeling_llama.py::rotate_half` + `llama.cpp/ggml_rope_impl`.\n\nSymptom before fix: model sampled near-uniform low-id tokens (byte-fallback territory) regardless of prompt — Q and K were being rotated along the wrong axis-pairs, so attention scores didn't correlate prompt and continuation.\n\nFix is a 2-line change to the offset computation:\n```rust\n// before (interleaved):\nlet off_lo = base + 2 * i;\nlet off_hi = base + 2 * i + 1;\n// after (half-split):\nlet off_lo = base + i;\nlet off_hi = base + i + half;\n```\n\n\n`runtime/src/tensor.rs:rayzor_tensor_bmm` assumed `[batch, M, K]` row-major contiguous layout for both inputs and computed offsets via `batch_i * M * K * elem_size`. But GQAttention feeds it views from `.permute([1,0,2])` (turns Q from `[seqQ, numHeads, headDim]` into `[numHeads, seqQ, headDim]`) and `.transposeLast2()` (turns K from `[numHeads, seqK, headDim]` into `[numHeads, headDim, seqK]`). Both are **zero-copy** — strides change, storage doesn't — so the old bmm read garbage memory.\n\nFix walks per-batch using each input's actual `strides[0]`, `strides[1]`, `strides[2]`. Retains the F32 + contiguous-inner-dim SIMD axpy fast path; falls back to a strided scalar loop otherwise.\n\nEffect: outputs went from random tokens to real English fragments. Single biggest user-visible win of the session.\n\n\nllama-chat on Llama 3.2 1B Instruct Q4_K_M, raw prompt `\"The capital of France is\"`:\n- **Before bmm fix**: `'� band� of� of� of� of� is� of� the'` — random tokens, mostly byte-fallback\n- **After bmm fix**: `'� the� day.� The� company.� It.� It.'` — real English words, not yet semantically right\n\nSingle-prompt tests:\n- `\"Hello\"` → `'importdef � � � likle.� In� this� case,� the'`\n- `\"def fibonacci(n):\"` → `'\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\|||||||'` (code context: weird)\n- `\"Once upon a time\"` → `'Frenz� 1.� 1.�'`\n\nWith Llama 3 chat template wrapper `\u003c|start_header_id|\u003euser\u003c|end_header_id|\u003e\\n\\nWhat is the capital of France?\u003c|eot_id|\u003e...`:\n- → `'Questioned� by� default)� \\n� � Suggested� that'se� �\\end� � \\k'`\n\nReal words, no `Paris`. The model is in a \"noisy\" regime — tokens are English-ish, but not the right ones.\n\n\n1. **Cumulative precision drift through 16 layers** — F32 throughout, but every matmul accumulates. RMS of intermediate activations may be growing/shrinking through layers in ways that pull the LM head off the right token. A per-layer hidden-state magnitude trace (compare to HF reference) would isolate this.\n\n2. **Sample-side issue** — `ArgmaxSampler` picks the literal max. If two tokens have logits within 0.1 of each other, noise can flip argmax. The right comparison is to HF's argmax on the same prompt + GGUF.\n\n3. **GGUF chat-template metadata** — Llama 3 GGUFs ship a `tokenizer.chat_template` Jinja string in metadata. Our loader doesn't apply it; we just pass raw text. For Instruct models, the user-friendly path is to wrap input in the chat template automatically.\n\n4. **Numerical issue in softmax / scale** — possible but the numerics inspection from the prior snapshot (`logits in [-8.5, 8.0]`, `mean|v|=1.83`, no NaNs) suggests the chain is working, just with weak signal.\n\n5. **Q6_K row-by-row cumulative dequant noise** — embed has 1.03M Q6_K blocks. Per-block dequant matches Python reference exactly (verified in `bugs_gguf_q6k_and_f32_load`), but cumulative noise hasn't been compared to an HF reference. Unlikely to matter for the dominant signal but worth ruling out.\n\n\nSame as the snapshot — pixel-compare per-layer hidden state vs HF, single-token oracle tests, attention-strip sanity. The bmm fix means these tests are now meaningful (previously the bmm bug dominated everything else).\n\n\n709 lib + 78 runtime + 128/128 haxe + tiny-transformer all green.\n\n\n- [[project-llama-chat-coherence-status]] — Phase (c) opening snapshot. This memory is the follow-up.\n- [[bugs-dtype-enum-cross-file-pointer]] — the enum-shadowing workaround that the compiler fix here makes obsolete.\n- [[bugs-gguf-q6k-and-f32-load]] — load-time bugs that made the pipeline run at all.\n- [[bugs-array-map-float-axis-audit]] / [[feature-double-and-array-stringifier]] — f64-precision sweep.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_llama_chat_coherence_phase_c` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}